        "id": 55401,
        "name": "KingD21",
        "username": "KingD21",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/k/839c29/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2013-03-27T20:15:56.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I almost always play a new deck blind -- whether it be villian, hero, or enviornment.</p><p>Theme in the villian deck is usually apparant right away (or at the very least during the 1st turn)</p><p>Strategy for the Hero deck takes some time playing it blind -- but I find figuring out the combos midgame much MUCH more satisfying; plus being an ex-Magic player for ~10yrs, finding in-game (intended and unintended combos) come at me relatively frequent. &nbsp;I think most CCG players feel the same way.</p><p>Eniornment deck is a toss up -- mainly b/c some hate you and others do not, but I like the idea of not knowing what could happen.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>If playing blind is an option, I'll do it. &nbsp;And like most Eurogamers, I HATE random/chance determining games, but for some reason I love it in this game. &nbsp;Only thing I think suffers is picking the 'right' team of heros for a given villian -- no way to do that without spoiling the surprise. &nbsp;Usually, if I play a solo game -- I'll have a tank, a support, multi-damage giver, and a rival vs. a villian [typically gives a fair fight on normal given all the unknowns]. &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>I still have nightmares from the crow onslaught...</p>",
